.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt WATTLE-APPLICATIONS 7
.Os Wattle DEVEL
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm wattle-applications
.Nd how Wattle applications are laid out, built and installed
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An application is a directory containing an
.Ar info file ,
source files (in either Wattle or Zig)
and documentation.
.Xr wattle-apps 1
installs an application and
.Xr wattle-build 1
builds the artifacts that an application declares.
This page describes what an application contains and where its
files are installed.
.
.Sh VENDORING
Vendoring a dependency refers to the practice of copying into
the source tree of an application the Wattle source files that
the application uses.
Within the code,
vendored dependencies are imported using a relative import:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpimport \&./vendor/greetings\*(rp
.Ed
.Pp
Wattle’s philosophy is that dependencies that are Wattle source
files should be part of the application.
A vendored copy is the version the application was tested with,
it changes only when the application’s author changes it and
the application runs without any dependency needing to be installed.
.Pp
This has trade-offs.
An application includes a copy of each dependency and a fix
to a dependency reaches the application only when its author
updates the copy.
Wattle takes the view that an application’s author is,
most of the time,
in the best position to weigh those trade-offs.
Because of this
.Xr wattle-apps 1
does not provide a mechanism to install Wattle source libraries.
.Pp
The application lists what it vendors under
.Ql ":vendored"
in its info file,
and
.Ql "wattle deps vendor"
copies each entry into the application.
See
.Xr wattle-deps 1 .
An entry without a
.Ql ":ref"
uses the HEAD of the default branch of a Git repository each
time.
The copies belong to the project and may be edited,
and no record is kept of the revision they came from.
.
.Sh LAYOUT
An application may contain these files:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Ql "info\&.edn"
.Xc
The info file,
at the root of the application.
.It Xo
.Ql "hooks/init\&.wattle"  or
.Ql "hooks\&.wattle"
.Xc
The application script.
If both exist,
.Ql "hooks/init\&.wattle"
is used.
The rest of
.Ql "hooks"
is installed with it,
so the script can import the files beside it.
.El
.Pp
An application needs an application script or an info file that
declares
.Ql ":artifacts"
or
.Ql ":man" .
.
.Sh INFO FILE
The info file holds one map.
All keys are optional,
but
.Ql ":name"
and
.Ql ":group"
are needed to install an application:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Ql ":name"
.Xc
The name of the application.
It is one directory name and is unique across groups.
.It Xo
.Ql ":group"
.Xc
The group of the application,
by convention a reverse domain such as
.Ql "net\&.inqk" .
It is one directory name.
A directory name is not empty,
is not
.Ql "\&."
or
.Ql "\&.\&."
and contains no path separator.
.It Xo
.Ql ":vendored"
.Xc
A vector of maps that describe the sources that
.Ql "wattle deps vendor"
copies into the application.
Each has a
.Ql ":name"
and a
.Ql ":url" ,
and may have a
.Ql ":ref" ,
a
.Ql ":prefix"
and
.Ql ":paths" .
See VENDORING above and
.Xr wattle-deps 1 .
.It Xo
.Ql ":artifacts"
.Xc
A vector of maps that describe what
.Ql "wattle build"
builds.
Each has a
.Ql ":type"
and a
.Ql ":name" .
See
.Ql "Declarations"
in
.Xr wattle-build 1 .
.It Xo
.Ql ":man"
.Xc
A vector of the paths of man pages.
The section is the digit that ends the file name,
so
.Ql "man/greet\&.1"
is installed in
.Ql "man1" .
.El
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{:name \(dqgreeter\(dq
 :group \(dqnet\&.inqk\(dq
 :vendored \(lB{:name \(dqgreetings\(dq
             :url \(dqhttps://example\&.com/greetings\&.git\(dq
             :prefix \(dqdeps/greetings\(dq}\(rB
 :artifacts \(lB{:type :exe
              :name \(dqhello\(dq
              :entry \(dqmain\&.wattle\(dq}\(rB
 :man \(lB\(dqman/hello\&.1\(dq\(rB}
.Ed
.
.Sh INSTALLATION
An application with an application script is installed by running
the hooks that the script defines,
and nothing else.
An application without one is installed from its info file:
each
.Ql ":exe"
artifact is built with
.Xr wattle-build 1
and its program is installed in
.Ql "bin" ,
each
.Ql ":src"
artifact is installed as described below,
and each file in
.Ql ":man"
is installed in
.Ql "share/man" .
A
.Ql ":lib"
artifact is installed only by an application script or by a
.Ql ":src"
artifact that names it in
.Ql ":libs" ,
and a
.Ql ":web"
artifact is not installed.
.
.Ss Source programs
A
.Ql ":src"
artifact is a program that is installed as source.
Its
.Ql ":files"
are copied into the application directory.
A directory is copied without any entry named
.Ql "zig-out" ,
.Ql "\&.zig-cache" ,
.Ql "\&.wattle"
or
.Ql "\&.git" .
Each artifact in
.Ql ":libs"
is built with
.Xr wattle-build 1
in the application directory,
so that its native module is in
.Ql "zig-out/lib"
there.
This needs
.Ic zig
on the
.Ql "PATH" ,
and
.Ql "wattle apps upgrade"
builds the modules again.
.Pp
The program in
.Ql "bin"
has the name of the artifact and starts with
.Ql "#!/usr/bin/env wattle" .
It loads
.Ql ":entry"
and calls
.Ql "main"
with the path of the program followed by the arguments.
A bare
.Ql "import"
in the program searches
.Ql "deps" ,
.Ql "\&.wattle"
and
.Ql "zig-out/lib"
in the application directory.
Running the program needs
.Ic wattle
on the
.Ql "PATH"
and the prefix set with
.Ql "WATTLE_PREFIX" .
A program that needs neither is an
.Ql ":exe"
artifact.
Windows has no shebang lines,
so a
.Ql ":src"
artifact cannot be installed there.
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{:name \(dqgreeter\(dq
 :group \(dqnet\&.inqk\(dq
 :artifacts \(lB{:type :src
              :name \(dqgreet\(dq
              :entry \(dqmain\&.wattle\(dq
              :files \(lB\(dqmain\&.wattle\(dq \(dqlib\(dq \(dqdeps\(dq\(rB}\(rB}
.Ed
.
.Ss Hooks
The application script defines a function for each hook it uses.
Each takes the application’s manifest.
The hooks run in this order at installation:
.Pp
.Bl -enum -offset 3n -compact
.It
.Ql "clean" ,
only when a clean build is requested
.It
.Ql "build"
.It
.Ql "install"
.It
.Ql "check" ,
only when a check is requested
.El
.Pp
The
.Ql "remove"
hook runs when the application is removed.
.Pp
Within a hook,
these functions add files to the manifest,
so that
.Ql "wattle apps remove"
can remove them:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Ql "app/add-bin"
.Xc
Adds a program to the binpath.
.It Xo
.Ql "app/add-manpage"
.Xc
Adds a man page to the manpath.
.El
.Pp
Example of
.Ql "hooks/init\&.wattle" :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpdefn install
  \(lBmanifest &\(rB
  \*(lpapp/add-bin manifest \(dqhello\(dq\*(rp
  \*(lpapp/add-manpage manifest \(dqhello\&.1\(dq\*(rp\*(rp
.Ed
.
.Sh INSTALLED FILES
All locations are under
.Ql "*prefix*" ;
see
.Ql "WATTLE_PREFIX"
in
.Xr wattle 1 .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Ql "lib/wattle"
.Xc
The libpath,
which holds the applications.
.Ql "import"
does not search it.
See
.Ql "MODULES"
in
.Xr wattle 7 .
.It Xo
.Ql "lib/wattle/apps/<group>/<name>"
.Xc
The directory of an installed application:
.Ql "manifest\&.edn" ,
which lists its files and records its source and
.Ql ":rev" ,
the revision of the source,
a copy of its info file and a copy of its application script,
with the rest of its
.Ql "hooks"
directory.
The revision is the commit hash for a Git repository,
and
.Ql "sha256:"
and a hash of the files for a tarball or a directory.
.It Xo
.Ql "bin"
.Xc
The binpath,
for programs.
.It Xo
.Ql "share/man"
.Xc
The manpath,
for man pages.
.El
.
